00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:22.643
Yeah! But do we give up, no!

00:07:24.010 --> 00:07:26.990
- [Interviewer] Pronouns
should be on all name tags.

00:07:26.990 --> 00:07:30.000
Three, two, one, go.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:30.833
- Yeah

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:34.513
- Tricky one.

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:38.040
I have a question before
I make a decision,

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:42.170
what if the person hasn't
figured out their pronouns yet

00:07:42.170 --> 00:07:43.330
and they're uncomfortable
and it's not something

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:46.250
that they feel comfortable
assigning themselves to a label?

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:49.100
- You know the quickest way to
make those people comfortable

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:50.440
make it mandatory for everybody.

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:52.490
- No, but if somebody's on
their journey, it takes-

00:07:52.490 --> 00:07:53.650
- What if somebody hasn't figured it out?

00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:55.360
- If it was the norm, if it was the norm

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:56.670
for everyone to just have it.

00:07:56.670 --> 00:07:59.950
- I feel like that, I
feel, actually I'm gonna,

00:07:59.950 --> 00:08:03.150
I feel like making
someone label themselves

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:06.224
is what society's already trying to do.

00:08:06.224 --> 00:08:08.250
(in southern accent) Are
you a man? Are you a woman?

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:09.373
And you need to be.

00:08:09.373 --> 00:08:10.206
(in normal voice)

00:08:10.206 --> 00:08:11.810
I feel like making
somebody label themselves

00:08:11.810 --> 00:08:14.890
before they're ready
is counter productive.

00:08:14.890 --> 00:08:16.330
- I'm so torn on this one,

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:19.450
'cause I can honestly stand
at either end of this,

00:08:19.450 --> 00:08:21.000
and I feel strongly about both.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:23.100
- I think that we need
to afford people time

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:24.930
to really figure out who they
are, who they want to be,

00:08:24.930 --> 00:08:26.410
how they represent
themselves to the world.

00:08:26.410 --> 00:08:30.900
- The thought of having to identify

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:33.766
with a pronoun being
such a terrifying thing,

00:08:33.766 --> 00:08:36.850
goes to show how much we should dismantle

00:08:36.850 --> 00:08:38.360
the binary in the first place.
- Yeah.

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:39.320
- 'Cause like that's part of

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:41.170
why I identify with he/she/they,

00:08:41.170 --> 00:08:42.640
because I don't feel
that I've ever fit within

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:43.960
the binary at all, so that's why

00:08:43.960 --> 00:08:46.240
I feel really fluid to like
flip between all of them.

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:48.740
I'm personally really
tired of being misgendered.

00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:50.850
And I think other people
that live outside of

00:08:50.850 --> 00:08:52.160
the gender binary are sick of it.

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:54.580
So it's like, if you just
had it out there, it's like

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:56.420
it just makes it easier for people

00:08:56.420 --> 00:08:57.750
to like navigate something.

00:08:57.750 --> 00:09:01.910
- If they said, look,
there are no more he, she.

00:09:01.910 --> 00:09:04.190
There's only they, I
could subscribe to that.

00:09:04.190 --> 00:09:06.280
But I think if they are
forced to figure out a label,

00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:09.050
then I think we're forcing people to

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:11.100
do something that they may
not feel comfortable with.

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:12.930
- Like it's forcing somebody to have

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:15.460
a very public journey before
they may be ready for it.

00:09:15.460 --> 00:09:16.650
- And part of why I'm torn,

00:09:16.650 --> 00:09:19.840
maybe I might have like an
internal confusion that's going

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:21.020
on right now with sexual-
- But-

00:09:21.020 --> 00:09:24.170
- Hold on, one sec, with
sexuality and gender.

00:09:24.170 --> 00:09:27.537
'Cause for me sexuality,
like if I came out as gay

00:09:27.537 --> 00:09:29.330
and I felt like I couldn't take it back,

00:09:29.330 --> 00:09:33.300
but maybe to your point,
it's different with gender.

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:35.530
I just can't relate to it.

00:09:35.530 --> 00:09:38.640
- [Interviewer] Gender isn't important.

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:41.860
Three, two, one, go.

00:09:41.860 --> 00:09:42.693
- Wait?

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:45.890
What?

00:09:45.890 --> 00:09:47.670
Maybe I misunderstood that.

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:50.300
'Cause to me, like when I
think of "is gender important"?

00:09:50.300 --> 00:09:53.660
No, it's not, not in a way
that it's not important

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:54.493
to think about.

00:09:54.493 --> 00:09:56.780
For someone's journey,
gender is important,

00:09:56.780 --> 00:10:00.630
but like someone's definition
of gender isn't necessarily.

00:10:00.630 --> 00:10:03.340
- For some people, gender
is incredibly important

00:10:03.340 --> 00:10:08.300
to them and that helps
dictate who they are

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:09.480
and who they get to be,

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:11.170
and how they get to feel most comfortable.

00:10:11.170 --> 00:10:13.830
- I think there's been too
much emphasis put on gender,

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:18.080
especially by commercialism
of blue is for boys,

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:21.050
pink is for girls and this
is what defines gender.

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:23.180
I think there's been
too much defining gender

00:10:23.180 --> 00:10:24.410
and I think that gender should be fluid

00:10:24.410 --> 00:10:26.970
in whatever you feel
inside, which is why to me,

00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:28.190
I don't feel it's important.

00:10:28.190 --> 00:10:30.690
- But because for some
people it is important,

00:10:30.690 --> 00:10:33.773
we have to respect that, and
therefore it is important.

00:10:34.890 --> 00:10:38.120
As a globe of humans.

00:10:38.120 --> 00:10:38.953
Does that make sense?

00:10:38.953 --> 00:10:41.938
- Yes, I strongly believe
that it's very important,

00:10:41.938 --> 00:10:44.090
because of all the violence against women

00:10:44.090 --> 00:10:44.923
and the violence against trans people

00:10:44.923 --> 00:10:46.660
and the violence it's like, so for me,

00:10:46.660 --> 00:10:48.130
I feel like it's very important

00:10:48.130 --> 00:10:49.936
because until we can dismantle

00:10:49.936 --> 00:10:54.000
this oppressive, murderous gender binary

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:56.210
that we are living with, I feel like I-

00:10:56.210 --> 00:10:58.057
- But Bobby's idea is the goal.

00:10:58.057 --> 00:10:58.890
- [Jonathan] It's the goal.

00:10:58.890 --> 00:11:00.050
- Yeah, to me I think of it as a goal,

00:11:00.050 --> 00:11:00.883
that no, it's not important.

00:11:00.883 --> 00:11:02.700
- That's what I'm saying,
I understand the intention.

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:04.690
- [Jonathan] I love your
intention. I think it's great.

00:11:04.690 --> 00:11:07.840
- [Interviewer] I had a hard
time accepting my sexuality.

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:08.960
- Did you say had?

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:10.860
- [Interviewer] Yes.
- Oh.

00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:13.283
- [Interviewer] Three, two, one, go.

00:11:17.448 --> 00:11:21.450
- I'm from a very conservative
community as you all know.

00:11:21.450 --> 00:11:25.130
And so, I had time reconciling
how it was gonna play out

00:11:25.130 --> 00:11:26.120
in my future,

00:11:26.120 --> 00:11:28.260
and so I always knew I was
gay, I just didn't know if

00:11:28.260 --> 00:11:29.520
it was something I was
ever going to be able

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:33.320
to action without being possibly killed.

00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:35.430
And so, so yeah, it was hard.

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:36.750
It was a hard time reconciling it,

00:11:36.750 --> 00:11:38.210
but I always knew I was gay.

00:11:38.210 --> 00:11:40.860
- Internally, I feel like I knew who I was

00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:43.150
and I felt like I knew what my truth was.

00:11:43.150 --> 00:11:45.746
I felt like the outside world had like

00:11:45.746 --> 00:11:47.370
a bigger problem with it.

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:49.020
- I struggled with it, I think.

00:11:49.020 --> 00:11:51.260
A little like post-puberty,
when I realized

00:11:51.260 --> 00:11:54.610
that I was attracted to boys and girls,

00:11:54.610 --> 00:11:56.840
honestly though, even
just before Queer Eye,

00:11:56.840 --> 00:11:58.660
I had a lot of people in my
life who just assumed that

00:11:58.660 --> 00:11:59.560
I was straight.

00:11:59.560 --> 00:12:02.230
Landing that job and having my life change

00:12:02.230 --> 00:12:04.100
and all of the jobs and
everything gave me this

00:12:04.100 --> 00:12:06.840
like confidence that I didn't have before.

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:09.240
- [Interviewer] Queer
representation in media

00:12:09.240 --> 00:12:11.090
has become way better.

00:12:11.090 --> 00:12:14.383
Three, two, one, go.

00:12:19.650 --> 00:12:20.483
- Yeah.

00:12:24.200 --> 00:12:25.770
- The fact that I'm allowed on TV

00:12:25.770 --> 00:12:27.870
and I don't mean allowed as in
somebody gave me permission,

00:12:27.870 --> 00:12:30.290
but the fact that there's
somebody like me on TV,

00:12:30.290 --> 00:12:32.470
blows my mind. I never
thought in my lifetime

00:12:32.470 --> 00:12:35.050
a Muslim, queer, brown
person would be on TV.

00:12:35.050 --> 00:12:35.883
- With perfect hair.

00:12:35.883 --> 00:12:37.250
- With gorgeous hair.
(all laugh)

00:12:37.250 --> 00:12:38.550
- Yeah, when I was little the only person

00:12:38.550 --> 00:12:39.970
that was out was Ellen,

00:12:39.970 --> 00:12:41.850
and obviously that didn't
end well for her then.

00:12:41.850 --> 00:12:44.770
And then Will and Grace, that
wasn't even when I was little,

00:12:44.770 --> 00:12:46.550
that was still like early twenties.

00:12:46.550 --> 00:12:50.540
And I still think there's
a lot of broadcast shows

00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:53.630
that there's zero representation
in and there won't be

00:12:53.630 --> 00:12:56.210
because that demographic
won't be okay with it.

00:12:56.210 --> 00:12:58.710
- It still makes headlines
whenever there's like

00:12:58.710 --> 00:13:02.020
a queer story that's at the
forefront of the leading people.

00:13:02.020 --> 00:13:03.950
Whether it's people of
color, anybody queer,

00:13:03.950 --> 00:13:05.510
it still makes like a big splash,

00:13:05.510 --> 00:13:07.310
and it's like treated
as it's like a big deal,

00:13:07.310 --> 00:13:09.000
so it's not normalized yet.

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:10.820
- There are still movies being banned

00:13:10.820 --> 00:13:11.840
in Middle Eastern countries.

00:13:11.840 --> 00:13:13.980
- Even just like here,
we have like so many,

00:13:13.980 --> 00:13:16.750
you know, state legislatures
that are trying to ban books,

00:13:16.750 --> 00:13:18.900
'cause they're silly,
silly, silly sillies.

00:13:20.050 --> 00:13:22.490
- [Interviewer] We are
truly making a difference

00:13:22.490 --> 00:13:23.700
in the world.

00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:26.580
Three, two, one, go

00:13:32.970 --> 00:13:34.880
- Getting picked up from my house,

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:37.210
on the way to the
airport yesterday morning

00:13:37.210 --> 00:13:40.810
a 70-year-old white man driver pulls up,

00:13:40.810 --> 00:13:44.257
and I get in the car and
he looks at me and he goes,

00:13:44.257 --> 00:13:49.257
"I just have to say your show
has changed my family's life.

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:53.170
My daughter's 27 and she is transitioning

00:13:53.170 --> 00:13:55.620
and we couldn't relate,
we couldn't understand.

00:13:55.620 --> 00:13:57.100
And we watched your show,

00:13:57.100 --> 00:14:01.360
and it allows us to be able
to understand our daughter

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:02.660
and what she is going through,

00:14:02.660 --> 00:14:05.072
and you guys have helped my family heal

00:14:05.072 --> 00:14:07.840
and be there for our
daughter when there's no way

00:14:07.840 --> 00:14:09.190
we could have before."

00:14:09.190 --> 00:14:13.060
Moments like that really
make me proud of what we do,

00:14:13.060 --> 00:14:17.870
and make anything that, you
know, hot days, this and that.

00:14:17.870 --> 00:14:19.310
- [Antoni] Yeah.

00:14:19.310 --> 00:14:20.143
- Make it all worth it.

00:14:20.143 --> 00:14:21.860
- [Jonathan] Cause the reason
that I stopped on "somewhat",

00:14:21.860 --> 00:14:23.710
is I was like, oh, but
there's always more.

00:14:23.710 --> 00:14:24.720
We could always be doing more.

00:14:24.720 --> 00:14:26.590
We could always be making more progress.

00:14:26.590 --> 00:14:29.690
But I feel like what we're all doing,

00:14:29.690 --> 00:14:30.660
what we're all bringing.

00:14:30.660 --> 00:14:33.630
I do feel like we are
changing the perceptions

00:14:33.630 --> 00:14:36.260
and I do feel like we're
making a difference.

00:14:36.260 --> 00:14:38.060
And I feel really strongly about that.

00:14:38.060 --> 00:14:40.300
- Out of all the human
interactions that we've had,

00:14:40.300 --> 00:14:41.920
and it's like, yes, I get very overwhelmed

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:42.980
when people stop me.

00:14:42.980 --> 00:14:44.670
But like it never gets old when you see

00:14:44.670 --> 00:14:48.180
like multiple generations
of families stop you

00:14:48.180 --> 00:14:50.500
at an airport and be like, this
is a show that we can watch

00:14:50.500 --> 00:14:51.333
as a family.

00:14:51.333 --> 00:14:53.960
I never thought I was gonna
be part of that narrative.

00:14:53.960 --> 00:14:55.300
I'm still shocked by it.

00:14:55.300 --> 00:14:58.640
- I know it may seem arrogant to suggest

00:14:58.640 --> 00:15:01.150
that the work that we're
doing impacts the world

00:15:01.150 --> 00:15:02.280
and makes a difference.

00:15:02.280 --> 00:15:04.937
However, having kids from
Pakistan message to say,

00:15:04.937 --> 00:15:06.530
"I never believed there
was a future for me.

00:15:06.530 --> 00:15:08.810
I thought maybe that by
the time I hit my twenties

00:15:08.810 --> 00:15:09.890
which is when we're meant to get married,

00:15:09.890 --> 00:15:11.050
I'd have to kill my self."

00:15:11.050 --> 00:15:13.280
To know that you are out there.

00:15:13.280 --> 00:15:14.320
You've got a partner.

00:15:14.320 --> 00:15:16.591
You've now got a child
you've lived a life that you

00:15:16.591 --> 00:15:18.780
that was normal for you

00:15:18.780 --> 00:15:20.232
and your country is in a place that

00:15:20.232 --> 00:15:24.610
I dream our country will be
that kind of inspiration.

00:15:24.610 --> 00:15:25.680
I, it is priceless.

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:27.990
I don't think that any
of us can really quantify

00:15:27.990 --> 00:15:30.990
what impact we make on the
kids in villages in Pakistan,

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:32.766
who've never, ever, ever believed

00:15:32.766 --> 00:15:34.710
that they have a life past 20.

00:15:34.710 --> 00:15:35.610
That blows my mind.

00:15:35.610 --> 00:15:37.063
That's changing the world.

00:15:37.063 --> 00:15:38.774
(all cheering)

00:15:38.774 --> 00:15:40.781
- Come on, come here, Jackie.

00:15:40.781 --> 00:15:43.868
(indistinct talking)

00:15:43.868 --> 00:15:44.820
- Karamo, we're thinking,

00:15:44.820 --> 00:15:45.794
I don't know why I looked up.

00:15:45.794 --> 00:15:48.243
Karamo, we're thinking about you.

00:15:48.243 --> 00:15:49.324
- Gone too soon.

00:15:49.324 --> 00:15:50.160
- Do you wanna make it clear?

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:51.930
Karamo's just not feeling very well.

00:15:51.930 --> 00:15:54.081
He's back, we are still the Fab Five.

00:15:54.081 --> 00:15:55.380
(all cheering)

00:15:55.380 --> 00:15:56.350
- Watch the lights!

00:15:56.350 --> 00:15:57.183
- Whoops.

00:15:58.370 --> 00:16:00.360
I said "ow", but it didn't hurt.

00:16:00.360 --> 00:16:01.560
- [All] Thank you.

00:16:01.560 --> 00:16:03.060
- [Jonathan] Thanks, everyone.

